Method 1 (Using diagrams)

1. The denominator shows into how many parts one whole must be divided. Represent this in a diagram. In this case, there must be 10 parts.

![](https://electricbookworks.github.io/siyavula-open-textbooks-images/items/images/web/ebw-jss1-06-007.png)

2. Shade the number of parts shown by the first numerator. The first numerator is 7.

![](https://electricbookworks.github.io/siyavula-open-textbooks-images/items/images/web/ebw-jss1-06-008.png)

3. Now also shade the number of parts shown by the second numerator. The second numerator is 1. We must shade another 1 part.

![](https://electricbookworks.github.io/siyavula-open-textbooks-images/items/images/web/ebw-jss1-06-009.png)

4. Write the total shaded part as a fraction:

$$
\frac{8}{10}
$$

5. Write the fraction in its simplest form.

$$
\frac{8\div 2}{10\div 2}=\frac{4}{5}
$$

6. The full calculation is:

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 \phantom{00}\frac{7}{10}+\frac{1}{10}\newline
 =\frac{8}{10} 
 =\frac{4}{5}
 \end{array}
$$

Method 2 (Calculating directly)

1. The denominator shows into how many parts one whole must be divided. Write down the denominator.

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 \phantom{00}\frac{7}{10}+\frac{1}{10}\newline
 =\frac{\phantom{00000}}{10} 
 \end{array}
$$

2. Write down the numerators that must be added.

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 \phantom{00}\frac{7}{10}+\frac{1}{10}\newline
 =\frac{7+1}{10} 
 \end{array}
$$

3. Add the numerators.

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 \phantom{00}\frac{7}{10}+\frac{1}{10}\newline
 =\frac{7+1}{10} 
 =\frac{8}{10} 
 \end{array}
$$

4. Simplify the answer.

$$
\begin{array}{l}
 \phantom{00}\frac{7}{10}+\frac{1}{10}\newline
 =\frac{7+1}{10} 
 =\frac{8}{10} 
 =\frac{4}{5}
 \end{array}
$$

